  "canvas.generatePrompt":
    "Review the current project's code and structure, then create a polished, information-dense structured Canvas only when the user wants a standalone analytical result. Use it for codebase maps, architecture reviews, audits, data-heavy findings, comparisons, flows, timelines, and risks; skip it for targeted implementation, debugging, or another specific deliverable. Prefer a concise summary, 2-4 high-signal stats when supported, sections for relationships/flow and risks, file references with line numbers, and compact tables only for useful comparisons. Keep the block order hierarchical, ground every claim in inspected project evidence, omit empty or speculative blocks, and do not call the tool if there is no real content. Make every table self-describing with specific column names and units, source, or time range when applicable. Call the Canvas tool from the current tool list exactly once: native MCP uses canvas_create; the built-in BYOK agent may expose its canonical name as canvas.create. Never create, write, or open any Canvas file yourself — Code Work renders it in the right panel; if the Canvas tool is unavailable, say so. Use only non-empty sections, stats, file references, and tables; never replace the Canvas with a markdown table or generate executable UI code. Use a concise title and canvasId in the current system language.",
